# yes-toto — Project Overview

## What This Project Is

`yes-toto` is a faithful CLI replica of the GNU Core Utilities `yes` command.
It outputs a string (default: `y`) followed by a newline, in an infinite loop, to stdout — until stopped with Ctrl+C (exit `130`) or a broken pipe closes stdout (exit `0`).

On a **terminal**, output is one line per loop (like GNU `yes`). On a **pipe**, a 64 KiB prefill slab is used for throughput.

## Binary Name

The compiled binary must always be named **`yes-toto`**. Never rename it.
On Windows, MinGW may also emit `yes-toto.exe`; that is the same program.

## Platforms

| Platform | Build environment | Notes |
|----------|-------------------|-------|
| **Linux** | `gcc`/`clang` + GNU Make | Full POSIX path: `sigaction(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN)` |
| **Windows** | **MSYS2 UCRT64** (recommended) | Native Windows `.exe`; `install_sigpipe_ignore()` is a no-op under `_WIN32` |
| **Git Bash** | Works if `/c/msys64/ucrt64/bin` is **before** Anaconda on `PATH` | Same binary as UCRT64; see `toolchain-structure.mdc` |

Binaries are **not portable** across OSes — build separately on Linux and on Windows.

## Scope — Keep It Minimal

Supporting behaviour:

- Built-in `--help` / `--h` and `--version` / `--v` (detected anywhere in `argv`; help wins if both appear)
- GNU-compatible strings: tokens such as `--foo` or `-` are ordinary output, not errors

**Do not add**:

- Interactive or TUI modes
- stdin reading
- Logging or debug output in normal operation
- Single-dash `-h` / `-v` (only `--help`, `--h`, `--version`, and `--v` are special)
- Any feature that would break GNU `yes` behavioural compatibility

## Behavioural Contract

| Invocation | Output |
|---|---|
| `yes-toto` | prints `y\n` forever (one line per write); Ctrl+C → exit `130` |
| `yes-toto hello` | prints `hello\n` forever |
| `yes-toto a b c` | prints `a b c\n` forever (args joined with space) |
| `yes-toto ""` | prints `\n` forever |
| `yes-toto \| head -n5` | prints 5 lines; exits `0` on broken pipe (64 KiB pipe path) |
| `yes-toto --version` / `yes-toto --v` | prints `yes-toto <version>\n`, exits `0` |
| `yes-toto --help` / `yes-toto --h` | prints usage on stdout, exits `0` |

## Exit Codes

| Code | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `0` | `--help`, `--version`, or broken pipe (`EPIPE` on `write`) |
| `1` | Other `write()` failure, OOM building the joined line, or signal setup failure |
| `130` | Ctrl+C / `SIGINT` (`YES_TOTO_EXIT_SIGINT`, `128 + 2`) |

